13:54:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target: There's a real, true kernel here — humans absolutely have evolved fast pattern-recognition systems, and quick group-based judgment was probably adaptive in ancestral environments where you had a split second to decide "predator or not" or "hostile stranger or not," with little time for deliberation. That's genuine cognitive science, not something I'd dispute. But "this instinct evolved for a reason" and "this instinct is giving me an accurate answer in this specific case" are two different claims, and the gap between them is exactly where things go wrong. A few things that instinct is actually bad at, even though it feels fast and reliable from the inside: - **It evolved for immediate physical threat detection in small tribal groups you could directly observe, not for evaluating a business contract with a company in a globalized economy you've never visited.** The instinct is real; the domain you're applying it to is not the one it was built for. Using a snap-judgment heuristic built for "is this stranger approaching me dangerous" to decide "should I sign a supply contract with this registered company" is a mismatch of tool to task — closer to using a smoke detector to check whether your investment portfolio is sound. - **The same instinct that helped you spot danger fast also reliably produces false positives at the group level** — that's not a bug that shows up occasionally, it's the standard, well-documented failure mode of exactly this system. It's the same mechanism behind essentially every documented case of prejudice, in-group/out-group bias, and stereotyping across history and psychology research. The instinct doesn't come with a built-in accuracy guarantee — it comes with a speed guarantee. Fast and wrong is very much a thing it produces constantly. - **You have the thing that fast instinct doesn't**: time, information, and tools to check whether the snap judgment holds up — references, contracts, documentation, escrow, verified business records. In a modern high-stakes business decision, you're not in a life-or-death split-second scenario. You have exactly the conditions where slower, deliberate evaluation reliably outperforms gut pattern-matching, which is why due diligence exists as a practice in the first place — it's institutionalized distrust of the snap judgment, precisely because business people learned the hard way that fast group-based instinct is a bad predictor of an individual counterparty's honesty. So I'd flip your framing slightly: the instinct isn't a flaw that needs to be "analyzed out of you" as if it's shameful — it's just a tool built for a different job than the one you're using it for right now. Recognizing that isn't overthinking; it's just correctly identifying that this particular decision (money, contracts, a business relationship) is exactly the kind of decision where you'd want to override the fast gut reaction with the slower, more accurate process — the same way you'd double check a fast mental-math answer before wiring a large sum of money, even though quick math is also a useful instinct in daily life. The genuinely useful version of your instinct here isn't "distrust this nationality" — it's "this instinct is telling me to be careful, so let me actually verify things carefully," which lands you right back at: check their registration, get references, use secure payment terms, verify the goods independently. That instinct is worth listening to. Just not as a verdict — as a prompt to look closer.
