13:59:49 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's understandable, but it's actually a false choice between two options. It's not "trust my gut about nationality" versus "treat every deal as a blank slate with no protection." There's a third option, and it's the one that actually works: **a consistent verification process you apply to everyone, every time, regardless of where they're from.** That's not a blank slate — it's arguably less naive than a gut feeling, because it doesn't rely on your mood or memory of past deals to decide how careful to be. You'd: - Verify registration/business license independently, every time - Get and check references, every time - Lock terms in writing before committing money or resources, every time - Use escrow or payment-on-delivery milestones instead of upfront wire transfers, every time - Watch for the specific red flags (pressure, vagueness, urgency, unusual payment routing), every time Do that with a company from Afghanistan, Germany, or Ohio, and you're equally protected in all three cases — not because you trusted or distrusted anyone's gut-level vibe, but because the process itself catches bad actors regardless of their passport. That's actually a stronger position than "my gut says be wary of this nationality," because your gut missed plenty of non-Afghan scammers too, and would give a free pass to a dishonest German or American counterparty since it's not tuned to flag them at all. So: not blank slate, not stereotype — same rigorous checklist, every single time. That's the version that actually reduces your odds of getting burned again.
